"""Side-by-side Python 3 and modern JavaScript reference examples."""

# Each example is standalone so readers can run it without another row's state.
COMPARISONS = [
    ('Variables', 'x = 10\nname = "John"\nage = 25\nPI = 3.14', 'let x = 10;\nlet name = "John";\nlet age = 25;\nconst PI = 3.14;', 'Python uses assignment and uppercase names for constants by convention. JavaScript let allows reassignment; const prevents rebinding, but does not freeze arrays or objects.'),
    ('Print output', 'print("Hello")', 'console.log("Hello");', 'Both write output. Console representations of arrays and objects differ between runtimes.'),
    ('Comments', '# Single-line comment\n# Multi-line explanations use\n# multiple comment lines.\n\ndef greet():\n    """A function docstring."""\n    return "Hello"', '// Single-line comment\n/* Multi-line\n   comment */', 'Python triple-quoted text is a string literal, not a comment. A string at the start of a function, class, or module can serve as its docstring.'),
    ('If / else', 'age = 20\nif age >= 18:\n    print("Adult")\nelif age >= 13:\n    print("Teen")\nelse:\n    print("Child")', 'const age = 20;\nif (age >= 18) {\n  console.log("Adult");\n} else if (age >= 13) {\n  console.log("Teen");\n} else {\n  console.log("Child");\n}', 'Python uses indentation and elif. JavaScript uses braces and else if. Both examples print Adult.'),
    ('For loop', 'for i in range(5):\n    print(i)', 'for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n  console.log(i);\n}', 'Both print 0 through 4. The stop value in range is excluded.'),
    ('While loop', 'i = 0\nwhile i < 5:\n    print(i)\n    i += 1', 'let i = 0;\nwhile (i < 5) {\n  console.log(i);\n  i++;\n}', 'Both print 0 through 4. Python has no ++ increment operator; use += 1.'),
    ('List vs array', 'nums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums.append(4)\nprint(nums)', 'const nums = [1, 2, 3];\nnums.push(4);\nconsole.log(nums);', 'Both mutate the collection to contain 1, 2, 3, 4. Python append returns None; JavaScript push returns the new length.'),
    ('Loop through values', 'nums = [1, 2, 3]\nfor num in nums:\n    print(num)', 'const nums = [1, 2, 3];\nfor (const num of nums) {\n  console.log(num);\n}', 'Use JavaScript for...of for values. for...in enumerates property names and is not the equivalent of Python list iteration.'),
    ('Dictionary vs object', 'person = {"name": "John", "age": 20}\nprint(person["name"])', 'const person = {name: "John", age: 20};\nconsole.log(person.name);', 'Both print John. A missing Python key raises KeyError; a missing JavaScript property typically returns undefined. Objects can also inherit properties.'),
    ('Hash map', 'count = {}\ncount["a"] = 1\ncount["b"] = 2\nprint(count["a"])\nfor key, value in count.items():\n    print(key, value)', 'const count = new Map();\ncount.set("a", 1);\ncount.set("b", 2);\nconsole.log(count.get("a"));\nfor (const [key, value] of count) {\n  console.log(key, value);\n}', 'dict and Map preserve insertion order. Python keys must be hashable; JavaScript Map accepts arbitrary values, comparing object keys by identity.'),
    ('Hash set', 'seen = set()\nseen.add(5)\nprint(5 in seen)', 'const seen = new Set();\nseen.add(5);\nconsole.log(seen.has(5));', 'Both test membership. Python {} is an empty dictionary, not a set. JavaScript Set iterates in insertion order; Python set order is not guaranteed.'),
    ('Functions', 'def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\nprint(add(2, 3))', 'function add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}\nconsole.log(add(2, 3));', 'Both return 5 for numeric inputs. Python uses def and an indented body; JavaScript uses function and braces.'),
    ('Lambda vs arrow function', 'square = lambda x: x * x\nprint(square(4))', 'const square = x => x * x;\nconsole.log(square(4));', 'Both print 16. Python lambdas contain a single expression. JavaScript arrows can have block bodies and capture this from their surrounding scope.'),
    ('String length', 'name = "John"\nprint(len(name))\nprint(len("😀"))', 'const name = "John";\nconsole.log(name.length);\nconsole.log("😀".length);', 'Both report 4 for John. Python reports 1 for this emoji; JavaScript reports 2 because length counts UTF-16 code units. Neither measurement reliably counts user-perceived characters.'),
    ('Membership', 'word = "apple"\nif "a" in word:\n    print("Found")\nprint(2 in [1, 2, 3])', 'const word = "apple";\nif (word.includes("a")) {\n  console.log("Found");\n}\nconsole.log([1, 2, 3].includes(2));', 'Use in for Python substring or collection membership. Use includes for JavaScript strings and arrays; JavaScript in checks property existence.'),
    ('List comprehension vs map', 'nums = [1, 2, 3]\nresult = [x * x for x in nums]\nprint(result)', 'const nums = [1, 2, 3];\nconst result = nums.map(x => x * x);\nconsole.log(result);', 'Both create a new collection containing 1, 4, 9 without modifying nums.'),
    ('Filter', 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nresult = list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, nums))\n# Also: [x for x in nums if x % 2 == 0]\nprint(result)', 'const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nconst result = nums.filter(x => x % 2 === 0);\nconsole.log(result);', 'Both retain 2 and 4. Python filter returns an iterator, so list materializes it. JavaScript filter returns an array.'),
    ('Sorting', 'nums = [2, 10, 1]\nnums.sort()\nprint(nums)\nnums.sort(reverse=True)\nprint(nums)', 'const nums = [2, 10, 1];\nnums.sort((a, b) => a - b);\nconsole.log(nums);\nnums.sort((a, b) => b - a);\nconsole.log(nums);', 'Both mutate in place. JavaScript defaults to string-based ordering, so numeric sorting needs a comparator. Python sort returns None; JavaScript sort returns the same array.'),
    ('Queue', 'from collections import deque\nqueue = deque()\nqueue.append(1)\nqueue.append(2)\nprint(queue.popleft())', 'const queue = [];\nqueue.push(1);\nqueue.push(2);\nconsole.log(queue.shift());', 'Both remove 1 first (FIFO). deque.popleft is O(1); array shift can require O(n) work. For large JavaScript BFS traversals, use a head index or deque implementation.'),
    ('Stack', 'stack = []\nstack.append(1)\nstack.append(2)\nprint(stack.pop())', 'const stack = [];\nstack.push(1);\nstack.push(2);\nconsole.log(stack.pop());', 'Both remove 2 first (LIFO). On an empty collection, Python pop raises IndexError; JavaScript pop returns undefined.'),
    ('String join', 'words = ["I", "love", "coding"]\nprint(" ".join(words))', 'const words = ["I", "love", "coding"];\nconsole.log(words.join(" "));', 'Both print I love coding. Python calls join on the separator and requires string elements; JavaScript calls it on the array and coerces elements.'),
    ('Split', 'sentence = "hello  world"\nprint(sentence.split(" "))\nprint(sentence.split())', 'const sentence = "hello  world";\nconsole.log(sentence.split(" "));\nconsole.log(sentence.trim().split(/\\s+/));', 'Explicit single-space splitting preserves the empty middle item in both languages. Python split() collapses whitespace; the JavaScript regex version shown handles this nonempty input similarly, but empty input needs a separate check.'),
    ('Enumerate / index and value', 'nums = [10, 20]\nfor i, num in enumerate(nums):\n    print(i, num)', 'const nums = [10, 20];\nnums.forEach((num, index) => {\n  console.log(index, num);\n});\n// For break/continue, use:\n// for (const [i, num] of nums.entries()) { ... }', 'Both print each index and value. JavaScript forEach cannot break out early and does not await async callbacks; use for...of when needed.'),
    ('Exception handling', 'try:\n    x = 10 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print("Error")\nfinally:\n    print("Done")', 'try {\n  const denominator = 0;\n  if (denominator === 0) {\n    throw new Error("Division by zero");\n  }\n  const x = 10 / denominator;\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log("Error");\n} finally {\n  console.log("Done");\n}', 'Both examples print Error then Done. JavaScript Number division 10 / 0 returns Infinity, so this example explicitly throws; it would not enter catch without that guard.'),
    ('Classes', 'class Person:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def greet(self):\n        print(self.name)\n\nperson = Person("John")\nperson.greet()', 'class Person {\n  constructor(name) {\n    this.name = name;\n  }\n  greet() {\n    console.log(this.name);\n  }\n}\nconst person = new Person("John");\nperson.greet();', 'Both print John. Python methods receive self explicitly; JavaScript uses this and new for construction. Detached JavaScript methods may lose their receiver.'),
    ('Equality and identity', 'print(1 == "1")  # False\na = [1]\nb = [1]\nprint(a == b)    # True: contents\nprint(a is b)    # False: identity', 'console.log(1 === "1"); // false\nconst a = [1];\nconst b = [1];\nconsole.log(a === b); // false: identity\nconsole.log(1 == "1"); // true: coercion', 'Prefer === in JavaScript to avoid coercion. Python list equality compares contents; JavaScript array equality compares references.'),
    ('Truthiness and logical operators', 'items = []\nprint(bool(items))  # False\nage = 20\nprint(age >= 18 and age < 65)\nprint(not items)', 'const items = [];\nconsole.log(Boolean(items)); // true\nconst age = 20;\nconsole.log(age >= 18 && age < 65);\nconsole.log(items.length === 0);', 'Empty lists and dictionaries are falsy in Python; empty arrays and objects are truthy in JavaScript. Python uses and/or/not; JavaScript uses &&/||/!.'),
    ('Conditional expression', 'age = 20\nlabel = "Adult" if age >= 18 else "Minor"\nprint(label)', 'const age = 20;\nconst label = age >= 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor";\nconsole.log(label);', 'Both select one of two values and print Adult. The condition appears in a different position.'),
    ('Division and remainder', 'print(7 / 2)    # 3.5\nprint(-7 // 2)  # -4\nprint(-7 % 2)   # 1', 'console.log(7 / 2);             // 3.5\nconsole.log(Math.floor(-7 / 2)); // -4\nconsole.log(-7 % 2);            // -1', 'Python // rounds down. Math.trunc would instead round toward zero. For these integers, Python modulo follows the divisor sign; JavaScript remainder follows the dividend sign.'),
    ('Slicing and copying', 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(nums[1:3])\ncopy = nums[:] \nprint(nums[-1])', 'const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nconsole.log(nums.slice(1, 3));\nconst copy = [...nums];\nconsole.log(nums.at(-1));', 'Both slices exclude the end index and both copies are shallow. JavaScript nums[-1] is a property lookup, not last-element indexing; use at(-1).'),
    ('Default arguments', 'def add(value, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\nprint(add(1))\nprint(add(2))', 'function add(value, items = []) {\n  items.push(value);\n  return items;\n}\nconsole.log(add(1));\nconsole.log(add(2));', 'Both calls use independent lists here. Python default values are evaluated once at definition, so use None for a fresh list. JavaScript default expressions run per call when an argument is undefined or omitted.'),
]

OPERATIONS = [
    ('Add last', 'arr.append(x)', 'arr.push(x)', 'Mutates the collection; return values differ.'),
    ('Remove last', 'arr.pop()', 'arr.pop()', 'Empty: Python raises; JavaScript returns undefined.'),
    ('Remove first', 'arr.pop(0) / queue.popleft()', 'arr.shift()', 'Use deque or a head index for large queues.'),
    ('Add first', 'arr.insert(0, x)', 'arr.unshift(x)', 'Front insertion shifts existing array/list elements.'),
    ('Length', 'len(arr)', 'arr.length', 'For Map and Set in JavaScript use .size.'),
    ('Contains', 'x in arr', 'arr.includes(x)', 'Object equality semantics differ.'),
    ('Dictionary / map', '{} / dict()', 'new Map() / {}', 'Map supports arbitrary key values; objects have string/symbol keys.'),
    ('Set', 'set()', 'new Set()', 'Unique values; Python elements must be hashable.'),
    ('Iterate map', 'for k, v in data.items():', 'for (const [k, v] of data) {}', 'JavaScript example assumes a Map; use Object.entries for objects.'),
    ('Reverse', 'arr.reverse()', 'arr.reverse()', 'Both mutate; Python returns None, JavaScript returns the array.'),
    ('Numeric sort', 'arr.sort()', 'arr.sort((a, b) => a - b)', 'Ascending in place; use copies to preserve input.'),
]

DSA_PATTERNS = [
    ('Two pointers', 'left += 1\nright -= 1', 'left++;\nright--;', 'Advance pointers through a sequence; often O(n) with O(1) extra space.'),
    ('Sliding window', 'counts = {}\nseen = set()', 'const counts = new Map();\nconst seen = new Set();', 'Track window frequencies or membership. Shrink while the window violates its constraint.'),
    ('BFS queue', 'from collections import deque\nqueue = deque([start])\nwhile queue:\n    node = queue.popleft()', 'const queue = [start];\nfor (let head = 0; head < queue.length; head++) {\n  const node = queue[head];\n}', 'Traversal skeletons: enqueue unseen neighbors and mark them visited. The JS head index avoids repeated shift; consumed entries remain allocated.'),
    ('DFS', 'stack = [start]\nwhile stack:\n    node = stack.pop()', 'const stack = [start];\nwhile (stack.length) {\n  const node = stack.pop();\n}', 'Push unvisited neighbors to complete traversal. Iterative DFS avoids recursion-depth limits in either language.'),
    ('Binary search', 'while left <= right:\n    mid = (left + right) // 2', 'while (left <= right) {\n  const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);\n}', 'Skeletons for sorted data: compare with the target and update a boundary every iteration, or return when found.'),
    ('Heap / priority queue', 'import heapq\nheap = []\nheapq.heappush(heap, 3)\nsmallest = heapq.heappop(heap)', '// Use a binary-heap implementation\n// or a priority-queue library.\n// API depends on that implementation.', 'Python includes heapq for min-heaps. Standard JavaScript has no built-in general-purpose priority queue; check the interview environment.'),
    ('Hash map', 'counts[value] = counts.get(value, 0) + 1', 'counts.set(value, (counts.get(value) ?? 0) + 1);', 'Frequency-counting snippets assume an existing dict / Map. Handle absent keys before incrementing.'),
    ('Hash set', 'if value not in seen:\n    seen.add(value)', 'if (!seen.has(value)) {\n  seen.add(value);\n}', 'Membership snippets assume an existing set / Set. Useful for duplicate detection and graph visitation.'),
]
